artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a federated learning protocol in which a server aggregates model updates contributed by a (potentially large and dynamic) population of client devices. To protect the contents of individual client updates during aggregation, the protocol uses a homomorphic encryption (HE) scheme: each client encrypts its update under a public key, the server homomorphically combines the ciphertexts (e.g., by adding them), and the resulting ciphertext is decrypted to reveal only the aggregate.

Assume the following realistic constraints on the federated setting:
(a) there is no permanently available, universally trusted external party that can be assumed not to collude with the server;
(b) clients are intermittently online, may drop out at any time, and the participating set changes from round to round;
(c) standard HE schemes used at scale (e.g., lattice-based schemes) require periodic refreshing/rotation of secret keys;
(d) any party that learns the secret key can decrypt individual ciphertexts and thus break privacy of single clients' updates;
(e) corrupted clients and a corrupted server may collude up to some bounded fraction.

Design and analyze a key-management and trust structure for the HE-based federated learning protocol that specifies (i) which entity or entities hold the decryption key material, (ii) how keys are generated, refreshed, and distributed across rounds with churning client participation, and (iii) a precise corruption/threshold model under which the protocol provably preserves the privacy of every honest client's individual update against the server and any colluding adversaries, without relying on a permanently trusted external decryptor.

Formally: produce a threshold (or otherwise distributed) HE-based federated aggregation protocol together with a security definition and proof, or establish an impossibility result, characterizing the trust assumptions necessary and sufficient to achieve client-level privacy of updates in this setting.

